Seraphim  <gme6@cornell.edu> wrote:
>Lars Kecke <kecke@physik.uni-freiburg.de> wrote in
>>C341-16 Lamps found in bones piles may behave oddly. Fixed in 3.4.2
>>and quite harmless IIRC. Don't do everything the game tells you.
>I thought that bug was related to the fact that the counter for lamps was 
>related to the turn they were lit on, so if I died on turn 100,000 left a 
>bones file with a lit lamp, and then found the lamp on turn 10,000 the 
>lamp would then burn for 90,000 turns.

Not quite; lamps were handled correctly. For most objects the "age" is the
turn the object was created on, so that the age need not be incremented
every turn. This requires a shift in the "age" when loading a bones file.
Objects like lamps that burn down use the "age" bytes differently; for the
charge remaining, which _is_ decremented every turn while the lamp is lit.

Hence there is a specific exclusion from the age-shift code for lamps and
lanterns, so they keep the same remaining charge when found in bones.
However, when the potion of oil was added, it did not receive this
exclusion, even though potions of oil do use "age" as "amount remaining"
in case you light them and then snuff them without using them as Molotov
cocktails.

Hence when oil was found in a bones file, refilling a lamp with it might
either give that lamp a vast charge or no charge at all; the latter case
resulted in a lamp that could be lit, but would then immediately revert
without any messages to an empty lamp. However, it would not produce the
messages originally complained of here.
